---
import IPOLayout from '../../layouts/IPOLayout.astro';
import IPOStatusSections from '../../components/IPOStatusSections.astro';
import { getAllIPOs } from '../../lib/data/ipos';
import { withCorrectStatuses } from '../../utils/ipo-status';
import { formatIpoTabSubtitle } from '../../lib/ipo-list-sections';

const iposData = withCorrectStatuses(await getAllIPOs());
const subtitle = formatIpoTabSubtitle(iposData, { type: 'sme', totalLabel: 'SME IPOs' });

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CollectionPage",
  "name": "SME IPOs India 2026",
  "description": "All SME IPOs on BSE SME and NSE Emerge with subscription and listing data.",
  "url": "https://ipofins.com/ipo/sme",
};
---

<IPOLayout title="SME IPOs India 2026 - BSE SME & NSE Emerge | IPOFins" description="All SME IPOs in India 2026. Track small and medium enterprise IPOs on BSE SME and NSE Emerge platforms." activeTab="sme" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="container-wide py-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mb-1">SME IPOs — 2026</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-surface-500 mb-6">{subtitle}</p>

    <IPOStatusSections
      ipos={iposData}
      listFrom="sme"
      type="sme"
      showIssueSize
      emptyMessage="No SME IPOs available currently."
    />
  </section>

  <!-- SEO Content -->
  <section class="container-wide py-12">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mb-4">What is an SME IPO?</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-surface-600 dark:text-surface-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          SME IPOs (Small and Medium Enterprise Initial Public Offerings) are public offerings by smaller companies listed on dedicated platforms — BSE SME and NSE Emerge. These companies typically have issue sizes between ₹5 Cr and ₹50 Cr, and may have shorter operating histories compared to mainboard companies. SME IPOs have gained immense popularity in India since 2022, with many delivering 100%+ listing gains.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-surface-800 dark:text-surface-200 pt-2">How to Apply for SME IPOs</h3>
        <p>
          The application process for SME IPOs is similar to mainboard IPOs, but with a key difference — the minimum lot size is higher (typically ₹1-2 lakh):
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Open your broker's app and navigate to the IPO section</li>
          <li>Select the SME IPO you want to apply for</li>
          <li>Note: Minimum investment is 1 lot (₹1-2 lakh, much higher than mainboard)</li>
          <li>Enter your UPI ID and submit. Approve the mandate on your UPI app.</li>
          <li>Allotment is typically announced within 5-7 days after close</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-surface-800 dark:text-surface-200 pt-2">Common Use Cases for Tracking SME IPOs</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Listing gains:</strong> SME IPOs frequently deliver 50-200% listing gains (but also carry loss risk)</li>
          <li><strong>Early-stage investing:</strong> Get in early on promising small companies before they grow</li>
          <li><strong>Subscription demand:</strong> SME IPOs can show very high oversubscription during the bidding period</li>
          <li><strong>Portfolio diversification:</strong> Exposure to small-cap and micro-cap segments</li>
          <li><strong>Sector-specific bets:</strong> Many niche sectors only have SME-listed companies</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-surface-800 dark:text-surface-200 pt-2">Risks of SME IPOs</h3>
        <p>
          SME IPOs carry significantly higher risk than mainboard IPOs. Key risks include: lower liquidity after listing (shares may hit upper/lower circuits), limited analyst coverage, shorter operating history, smaller promoter track record, and potential for price manipulation in low-volume scrips. SEBI has tightened SME listing norms in 2025 to address these concerns. Always research thoroughly before investing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <section class="container-wide py-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mb-6">SME IPO FAQs</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      <details class="card group" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-surface-900 dark:text-white">What is an SME IPO?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-surface-600 dark:text-surface-300">SME (Small and Medium Enterprise) IPOs are listed on BSE SME or NSE Emerge platforms. These are smaller companies with issue sizes typically ₹5–50 Cr. They have relaxed listing requirements compared to mainboard. SME IPOs can give very high listing gains (50–100%+) but carry higher risk due to lower liquidity and limited company track record.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card group">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-surface-900 dark:text-white">What is the minimum investment for an SME IPO?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-surface-600 dark:text-surface-300">SME IPOs have a minimum lot size of ₹1–2 lakh (higher than mainboard). For example, if lot size is 2000 shares at ₹60, minimum investment is ₹1,20,000. This higher minimum is set by SEBI to ensure only investors who can afford the risk participate in SME IPOs.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card group">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-surface-900 dark:text-white">Are SME IPOs riskier than Mainboard IPOs?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-surface-600 dark:text-surface-300">Yes, generally. SME companies are smaller, have shorter operating history, lower liquidity after listing, and less analyst coverage. However, some SME IPOs deliver exceptional returns (100%+ listing gains). Do thorough research — check promoter background, financials, industry outlook, and subscription trends before applying.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card group">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-surface-900 dark:text-white">How long does it take to sell SME IPO shares after listing?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-surface-600 dark:text-surface-300">You can sell on listing day itself. However, SME stocks have lower liquidity — there might not be enough buyers at your desired price. Many SME stocks hit upper/lower circuits on listing day. It's recommended to place limit orders rather than market orders. Lock-in for anchor investors is 30/90 days.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</IPOLayout>
